<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  OneBeat is NOT sandboxed, and cannot be.

  A plug-in host has to read /Library/Audio/Plug-Ins and ~/Library/Audio/Plug-Ins
  and then load and execute code out of them. The App Sandbox permits neither:
  there is no entitlement for the plug-in folders, and $HOME is redirected into a
  container the user's plug-in installers know nothing about. With the sandbox on
  (Flutter's macOS template turns it on), OB-2-02's scan finds exactly zero
  plug-ins in a shipped build, and ADR-003's out-of-process helper cannot be
  spawned the way it is designed either. This is why every shipping macOS DAW is
  distributed with Developer ID signing and notarization rather than through the
  Mac App Store.

  So this is a deliberate architectural position, not a shortcut around a
  permission error. What replaces the sandbox as the containment story:

    - plug-ins run in a separate helper process (ADR-003, OB-2-05), which is what
      actually protects the user's work from a plug-in crash (G3);
    - the helper is the only binary carrying disable-library-validation; the main
      app keeps library validation on;
    - no network entitlement, here or in the helper.

  OB-2-06 (Gate G-B) owns signing and notarization and re-checks this decision.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Hardened Runtime, which notarization requires, is set in the Xcode
	     project rather than here. These are the exceptions to it. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
